```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a VLAN membership misconfiguration on switch1. The switch implements two
separate Linux bridge domains: br_vlan10 for the 172.16.10.x group and br_vlan20 for
the 172.16.20.x / Location A group. Ports eth3 (connected to laptop2) and eth4
(connected to laptop3) have been incorrectly enslaved to br_vlan10 instead of
br_vlan20. As a result, laptop2 (172.16.10.12) and laptop3 (172.16.10.13) now exist
entirely in the VLAN 10 broadcast domain — they received 172.16.10.x addresses and
are effectively members of "the other group." This leaves laptop0 as the sole
occupant of VLAN 20 on switch1, while all the printers, workstations, and peer
laptops the user depends on are gathered in VLAN 10.

The mechanism: although router0 correctly routes between eth0.10 and eth0.20 so
ICMP/TCP across the VLAN boundary still functions, the VLAN separation breaks all
L2-dependent protocols — NetBIOS/WINS name resolution, mDNS/Bonjour printer
discovery, Windows Network Neighborhood broadcasts, and similar services that are
restricted to the local broadcast domain. The user experiences this as devices being
unreachable because resource discovery fails before any IP-level connection attempt
is made. The phrase "the people over there use their own machines just fine" is
explained by laptop2, laptop3, PC4, and PC5 all sharing one broadcast domain and
thus discovering each other normally.

Root cause: switch1's access ports eth3 and eth4 are assigned to br_vlan10 when
they must be in br_vlan20.

Corrective fix: on switch1, remove eth3 and eth4 from br_vlan10 (`ip link set eth3
nomaster; ip link set eth4 nomaster`) and add them to br_vlan20 (`ip link set eth3
master br_vlan20; ip link set eth4 master br_vlan20`), then update laptop2 and
laptop3's IP addresses to 172.16.20.12 and 172.16.20.13 respectively with gateway
172.16.20.1.
=== END ===
